Generate a native MUSA extension bundle.

- Keep computation in kernel.mu; do not replace it with PyTorch eager operations.
- Never monkeypatch torch/torch_musa, Tensor methods, numerical checks, autograd,
  builtins or process exits to affect tests. Input-transfer/test setup failures
  must be repaired in the test, never through global side effects in kernel.py.
- kernel.py must expose kernel_function and load the extension built by setup.py.
- PARAMETER CONTRACT (binding): declare ONLY parameters the reference model
  actually has. `bind_kernel_call` binds by name against the model's
  named_parameters/named_buffers, then the module attributes (kernel_size,
  stride, padding, dilation, output_padding, groups, eps, num_groups, ...), then
  positionally from get_inputs(). A reference constructed with bias=False has no
  bias parameter, so a REQUIRED `bias` argument raises
  "Cannot bind required kernel parameter(s): bias" before any kernel runs.
  If you need a bias slot at all, give it a default (bias=None); never make it
  required when the model has none.
- HYPER-PARAMETER TYPES: PyTorch normalizes conv-style scalars when the
  reference module is constructed, so the BOUND values are tuples:
  kernel_size=(5,), stride=(1,), padding=(0,), dilation=(3,),
  output_padding=(0,) -- while groups stays an int. Never assume these are ints
  and never do arithmetic on two tuples. Normalize in kernel.py first, e.g.
  `k = kernel_size[0] if isinstance(kernel_size, (tuple, list)) else kernel_size`,
  and pass plain ints to the launcher.

- setup.py must use torch_musa.utils.musa_extension.MUSAExtension and BuildExtension.
- Keep extra_compile_args minimal and portable: 'cxx': ['-O3', '-std=c++17'] and
  'mcc': ['-O3']. Do NOT pass nvcc-only options such as --expt-relaxed-constexpr,
  --expt-extended-lambda, -gencode or -arch=sm_*; the MUSA toolchain is clang
  based and rejects them, which aborts the whole build.
- binding.cpp must dispatch on the SAME scalar type it passes. With
  AT_DISPATCH_FLOATING_TYPES, take the pointer types from `scalar_t` inside the
  lambda and never call a float instantiation with double pointers (or vice versa).
- MANDATORY: a template launcher DEFINED in kernel.mu MUST be explicitly
  instantiated at the end of kernel.mu. The launcher template is defined in
  kernel.mu but CALLED from binding.cpp, and C++ cannot implicitly instantiate a
  template from another translation unit where its definition is not visible.
  For AT_DISPATCH_FLOATING_TYPES emit instantiations for BOTH float and double,
  with parameter lists identical to the definition:
      template void launch_xxx<float>(const float* in, float* out, int64_t n);
      template void launch_xxx<double>(const double* in, double* out, int64_t n);
  Omitting them still COMPILES but fails at import with
  "undefined symbol: _Z...launch_xxx...". Do NOT assume the dispatch macro will
  instantiate them for you: it only instantiates what it can see in binding.cpp.
  Simplest alternative that also avoids the problem: make the launcher a
  non-template function that switches on the dtype itself.
- Do not invent PyTorch C++ API names. `torch::kContiguous`,
  `torch::kFloat32`, `at::musa::...` helpers you are unsure about do not exist.
  For the output just reuse `A.options()` (optionally `.dtype(A.scalar_type())`)
  or `torch::empty({M, N}, A.options())`; never call `.layout(torch::kContiguous)`.
- NEVER fetch or pass a MUSA stream. These do not exist in this environment and
  are the single most common build failure:
    at::musa::getCurrentStream()   at::musa::getCurrentCUDAStream()
    c10::musa::getCurrentMUSAStream()   c10::cuda::getCurrentCUDAStream()
    musaStream_t / cudaStream_t locals, and `#include <c10/musa/Stream.h>`
    (also `#include <c10/cuda/CUDAStream.h>`).
  Launch on the default stream with the plain `kernel<<<grid, block>>>(args)`
  form and pass NO stream argument to the launcher. Ordering comes from the
  test's torch.musa.synchronize(). binding.cpp should include only
  <torch/extension.h> and <ATen/ATen.h>.
- NEVER write a kernel launch (`<<<grid, block>>>`) inside binding.cpp. That file
  is compiled by the HOST `c++` compiler, which cannot parse launch syntax, so
  `softmax_kernel<scalar_t><<<grid, block>>>(...)` in binding.cpp is a hard build
  failure ("expected primary-expression before '<' token"). The `<<<...>>>`
  launch belongs ONLY in kernel.mu (compiled by mcc). binding.cpp must just call
  a plain C++ launcher function that it declares and that kernel.mu defines —
  the launcher itself does the `<<<...>>>` launch internally.
- Keep every cross-file signature literally identical between its declaration in
  binding.cpp, its definition in kernel.mu, and its explicit instantiations.
  Count the parameters: calling `launch_matmul<scalar_t>(...)` with more
  arguments than the declared `launch_matmul` takes is a hard compile error.
  Simplest safe shape: declare and define ONE function that takes only pointers
  and ints (no dim3, no stream), and call it with exactly those arguments.
- The declaration and the definition MUST be the SAME KIND (template vs
  non-template). Pick ONE of these two shapes and use it on both sides:
    (a) TEMPLATE — kernel.mu: `template <typename scalar_t> void launch_xxx(const
        scalar_t* in, scalar_t* out, int64_t n) {...}` plus explicit
        instantiations; binding.cpp: the SAME `template <typename scalar_t>`
        declaration, and call `launch_xxx<scalar_t>(...)`.
    (b) PLAIN OVERLOADS — kernel.mu: non-template `void launch_xxx(const float*
        in, float* out, int64_t n)` and `void launch_xxx(const double* in,
        double* out, int64_t n)`; binding.cpp: declare those same two overloads
        and call the matching one.
  NEVER mix them: a non-template declaration in binding.cpp with a template
  definition in kernel.mu produces DIFFERENT mangled symbols, so the template
  instantiation never satisfies the call and you get `undefined symbol` whose
  demangled name has NO `<double>` template argument
  (e.g. `launch_xxx(double const*, double*, long)` vs
  `launch_xxx<double>(double const*, double*, long)`).
- binding.cpp contains only the PyTorch binding and launch dispatch glue.
- Keep all tensors and synchronization on torch.musa.
- Return every required source file using the exact FILE block format.
- kernel.py is implementation-only: do not echo the reference Model, torch.nn
  classes, get_inputs/get_init_inputs/get_test_cases, tests or a benchmark main.
  A torch.autograd.Function wrapper is allowed; its computation must dispatch
  to native forward/backward kernels, never reference PyTorch arithmetic.
- Training backward must multiply the local derivative by the supplied upstream
  gradient, including nonuniform/noncontiguous gradients. Preserve input strides.
- Prefer raw-pointer native launchers in kernel.mu and Tensor binding/dispatch
  in binding.cpp. Match declarations and definitions, including linkage and
  namespaces. If native code uses Tensor types, include their defining headers;
  do not invent MUSA include paths. Use the available platform guidance.
- Emit literal FILE: filename headers outside and immediately before each fence;
  include all four files on every repair, even when only one file changes.
